theory.intro:
A **reciprocal model** $y=\frac{k}{x}$ describes inverse variation: as one quantity grows, the other shrinks. Its graph is a **hyperbola**, and the constant $k$ is the product $xy$, which is the same for every pair of values.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0.9:5.9,samples=100,variable=\x,<->] plot ({\x},{3.6/\x});
\node[right] at (2.3,2.5) {$y=\frac{k}{x}$};
\node[below] at (3.6,-0.2) {never touches the axes};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
The time $t$ hours for a $120$ km trip at an average speed of $s$ km/h is modelled by $t=\frac{120}{s}$, and the graph is shown. Use the graph to find the time taken at $40$ km/h.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=8cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-3,xmax=74,ymin=-1,ymax=14.5,xtick={0,10,...,70},ytick={0,1,...,13},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\scriptsize,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$s$},ylabel={$t$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=9.24:72,samples=120] {120/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

